  <!-- SECTION 2: WHAT IS DHT -->
  <div class="vy-article vy-section">
    <p class="vy-lead-in">If your hairline is creeping back, or your crown is thinning while the sides stay thick, that is not random shedding.</p>
    <p>It follows a predictable pattern, and that pattern has a name: androgenetic alopecia, better known as male pattern hair loss. It affects the majority of men at some point in their lives, and it is driven primarily by a single hormone.</p>

    <figure class="vy-figure-ls">
      <img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-386-simply-revival/images/scalp-closeup.jpg" alt="Close-up of the crown and part of a man's scalp" width="1600" height="900">
      <figcaption>Pattern hair loss shows up first where DHT-sensitive follicles are concentrated: the crown and the temples.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>What DHT Is, and Why It Causes Pattern Hair Loss</h2>
    <p>DHT, short for dihydrotestosterone, is a hormone your body makes naturally from testosterone. It plays a useful role earlier in life, but in adulthood it keeps circulating, and in men who carry a genetic sensitivity to it, DHT starts working against the hair on top of the head.</p>
    <p>Here is the mechanism in plain language: <b>DHT binds to receptors in your hair follicles</b>, especially at the temples and the crown. Each time an affected follicle completes a growth cycle, it comes back slightly smaller. The hairs it produces get shorter, thinner, and lighter. Think of the follicle as slowly shrinking with each cycle, until eventually it stops producing visible hair at all.</p>

    <figure class="vy-figure-ls">
      <img src="https://storage.googleapis.com/vysta/lp-386-simply-revival/images/followup-diagram.jpg" alt="Diagram comparing a shrunken hair follicle to a healthy hair follicle" width="1280" height="720">
      <figcaption>Follicle miniaturization: with repeated DHT exposure, a sensitive follicle gradually shrinks and produces thinner, weaker hair.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="vy-callout">
      <h4>Why it's called "pattern" hair loss</h4>
      <p>The follicles at your temples and crown carry more DHT-sensitive receptors than the follicles at the back and sides of your head. That is why hair loss shows up at the hairline and crown first, and why the band of hair around the sides usually stays put.</p>
    </div>

    <p>To be clear, DHT is not behind every type of hair loss: stress, nutrition gaps, thyroid issues, and certain medications can all contribute. But for the classic receding-temples-and-thinning-crown pattern, DHT sensitivity is the primary driver, which is exactly why it has become the primary target.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 3: WHY DHT BLOCKERS -->
  <div class="vy-article vy-section">
    <h2>Why Blocking DHT Is the Logical Response</h2>
    <p>Once you understand the mechanism, the logic follows naturally. Many hair products work downstream of the problem: thickening shampoos coat the hairs you still have, and stimulating treatments try to increase blood flow to the follicle. Those approaches can have their place, but none of them touch the trigger itself.</p>
    <p>A DHT blocker addresses the cause upstream. <b>Less DHT activity at the follicle means less of the shrinking process</b> that causes pattern hair loss in the first place. That is why, if the goal is to slow genetic hair loss rather than mask it, addressing DHT directly is the most targeted approach available.</p>
    <p>"DHT blocker" is a category, not a single product. It spans prescription medication (finasteride is the best-known) as well as natural, non-prescription formulas built around botanicals like saw palmetto. Both routes aim at the same target in different ways, and each comes with its own trade-offs.</p>

